Hey Believe Nation, thank
you so much for watching.

00:05:54.068 --> 00:05:56.400
If you want to learn
more about this subject,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.195
get a little bit deeper on it,

00:05:58.195 --> 00:05:59.788
I'm going to share some extra bonus clips

00:05:59.788 --> 00:06:01.469
that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:06:01.469 --> 00:06:03.422
- Just do it, just pick up a camera

00:06:03.422 --> 00:06:04.913
and start shooting something.

00:06:04.913 --> 00:06:06.704
Don't wait to be asked

00:06:06.704 --> 00:06:08.379
because nobody's going to ask you

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:10.300
and don't wait for the perfect conditions

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.989
because they'll never be perfect.

00:06:11.989 --> 00:06:13.621
It's a little bit like having a child.

00:06:13.621 --> 00:06:15.653
If you wait until the
right time to have a child,

00:06:15.653 --> 00:06:17.584
you'll die childless.

00:06:17.584 --> 00:06:20.408
And I think filmmaking is
very much the same thing,

00:06:20.408 --> 00:06:21.769
you just have to take the plunge

00:06:21.769 --> 00:06:23.369
and just start shooting something.

00:06:23.369 --> 00:06:25.050
Even if it's bad, you can always hide it,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.464
but you will learn something.

00:06:26.464 --> 00:06:29.565
- It was a lot harder to get
started when I was growing up

00:06:29.565 --> 00:06:30.851
and wanting to be a movie director,

00:06:30.851 --> 00:06:32.204
almost at 12 years old

00:06:32.204 --> 00:06:34.661
because when I first picked up a camera--

00:06:34.661 --> 00:06:35.954
- Where were you living when you first--

00:06:35.954 --> 00:06:38.103
- [Steven] Scottsdale.
- You were in Arizona?

00:06:38.103 --> 00:06:42.396
- I was looking for a way
to fulfill the requirements

00:06:42.396 --> 00:06:44.307
to get a photography merit badge.

00:06:44.307 --> 00:06:45.921
I was in the Boy Scouts of America.

00:06:45.921 --> 00:06:47.387
And--

00:06:47.387 --> 00:06:48.547
- Did you want to be a filmmaker before?

00:06:48.547 --> 00:06:50.037
- No, I didn't want to
be a filmmaker at all,

00:06:50.037 --> 00:06:51.497
I just wanted to be an Eagle Scout.

00:06:51.497 --> 00:06:52.767
That was my only aspiration,

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:56.795
I wanted to get the highest
rank I could in Scouting.

00:06:56.795 --> 00:06:59.257
And so on my way to being an Eagle Scout,

00:06:59.257 --> 00:07:01.986
I had to get a certain
requisite amount of merit badges

00:07:01.986 --> 00:07:03.091
and one of them I wanted
to get was photography.

00:07:03.091 --> 00:07:04.634
And a requirement was,

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:07.299
to tell a story with still photographs.

00:07:07.299 --> 00:07:08.981
And my Dad's camera broke,

00:07:08.981 --> 00:07:10.214
he did not have a still camera,

00:07:10.214 --> 00:07:12.600
like the Brownie Kodak that
he had, wasn't working.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.433
But he did have an 8mm
Kodak, 8mm movie camera

00:07:17.617 --> 00:07:18.904
and I went to the Scout Master

00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:20.889
and I said, can we change the rules,

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:22.656
just so I can tell a story on film?

00:07:22.656 --> 00:07:23.949
Because we don't have a still camera.

00:07:23.949 --> 00:07:25.472
And the Scout Master
said, go ahead and do it.

00:07:25.472 --> 00:07:28.914
So, I got three minutes worth of film,

00:07:28.914 --> 00:07:32.144
which is, by the way, all
those cameras used to take

00:07:32.144 --> 00:07:34.168
these little three minute rolls of film.

00:07:34.168 --> 00:07:37.263
And I made this Western on a weekend--

00:07:37.263 --> 00:07:38.375
- How did you edit?

00:07:38.375 --> 00:07:39.657
Did you camera cut?

00:07:39.657 --> 00:07:40.678
- In camera cut.

00:07:40.678 --> 00:07:41.866
You know a little shot,

00:07:41.866 --> 00:07:44.931
my friends in cowboy outfits
robbing a stagecoach,

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:47.068
which was sort of an
exhibit outside of place

00:07:47.068 --> 00:07:48.334
called Pinnacle Peak Patio.

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:51.341
A restaurant which is still
there, outside of Scottsdale.

00:07:51.341 --> 00:07:52.608
- How did you know to camera cut?

00:07:52.608 --> 00:07:53.924
- I just figured, you know,

00:07:53.924 --> 00:07:55.427
you do a shot in this direction,

00:07:55.427 --> 00:07:57.212
the bad guys with their guns and masks on,

00:07:57.212 --> 00:07:59.541
my sisters played the damsels in distress

00:07:59.541 --> 00:08:02.190
in the stagecoach, giving
away their costume jewelry,

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.360
well, my mother's jewelry.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.707
(laughing)

00:08:04.707 --> 00:08:06.120
I didn't tell we took my Mom's jewelry,

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.918
to give away to 12 year olds,

00:08:07.918 --> 00:08:10.184
you know, pretending to be robbers.

00:08:10.184 --> 00:08:11.589
And I made this little movie

00:08:11.589 --> 00:08:14.328
and then showed it to the
Boy Scouts troop that weekend

00:08:14.328 --> 00:08:15.474
and they all went crazy for it.

00:08:15.474 --> 00:08:16.760
They screamed and applauded.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.254
I suddenly got bitten by that bug

00:08:18.254 --> 00:08:20.426
and I wanted to do this
for the rest of my life.

00:08:20.426 --> 00:08:21.950
It was like, became so clear to me.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:24.444
But it was hard then
because to make movies,

00:08:24.444 --> 00:08:27.169
you had to pay for film, pay
for developing of the film.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:28.884
So, I had to go to my Dad to say,

00:08:28.884 --> 00:08:30.570
Dad, I'll earn some money

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.113
but I can't earn all the money,

00:08:32.113 --> 00:08:33.539
'cause I have to go to school.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:34.684
I can't get a job to earn the money

00:08:34.684 --> 00:08:36.498
to pay for all this equipment.

00:08:36.498 --> 00:08:37.765
So my Dad helped me.

00:08:37.765 --> 00:08:40.004
However, today it's easier.

00:08:40.004 --> 00:08:41.784
Today, everybody seems
to have a camcorder.

00:08:41.784 --> 00:08:44.692
Everybody has some high digital camera

00:08:44.692 --> 00:08:46.757
and it's a lot easier
to get your hands on--

00:08:46.757 --> 00:08:48.693
- You can buy that editing
program for the computer.

00:08:48.693 --> 00:08:50.655
- You can buy a software
program for the computer

00:08:50.655 --> 00:08:52.226
and go out and make a movie

00:08:52.226 --> 00:08:53.726
and tell a story and figure out

00:08:53.726 --> 00:08:55.735
who you are as a storyteller.

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:58.484
And so in a sense, it's easier today

00:08:58.484 --> 00:09:01.365
for young people to at
least prove to themselves

00:09:01.365 --> 00:09:03.242
what they want to do for
the rest of their lives

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:05.520
or at least in the interim
of the rest of their lives.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.809
And it's also, if you
get bitten by that bug,

00:09:07.809 --> 00:09:09.772
you can make a lot of little movies

00:09:09.772 --> 00:09:11.347
and some how, those films will get seen

00:09:11.347 --> 00:09:13.174
by people, hopefully, that will hire you

00:09:13.174 --> 00:09:15.029
to do a music video someday,

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:16.889
or a tv commercial or a television show,

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:18.358
or someday, a feature film.

00:09:18.358 --> 00:09:21.358
- I was very headstrong young person

00:09:23.545 --> 00:09:24.962
and by 1983 or 4,

00:09:27.941 --> 00:09:29.741
had started my own publishing company,

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:31.189
to publish my own books.

00:09:31.189 --> 00:09:33.528
Realizing that no one else will publish me

00:09:33.528 --> 00:09:35.381
and I was right about that.

00:09:35.381 --> 00:09:39.785
And so my first book, I saved
my food money from Black Flag,

00:09:39.785 --> 00:09:41.323
a little here, a little there,

00:09:41.323 --> 00:09:44.839
and I saved up enough to
make a fold and staple book.

00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:47.311
I couldn't type, so I
hand wrote out the things

00:09:47.311 --> 00:09:50.184
and I made a dummy that I could

00:09:50.184 --> 00:09:53.451
offset, print, fold, and staple.

00:09:53.451 --> 00:09:54.976
And I sold them for $2.

00:09:54.976 --> 00:09:57.324
Mainly gave them away, but I sold enough,

00:09:57.324 --> 00:09:59.543
and they blew out, 500 just went.

00:09:59.543 --> 00:10:03.457
And then I took that money to
make another print run of that

00:10:03.457 --> 00:10:05.142
and from that money,

00:10:05.142 --> 00:10:07.225
I made my first paperback

00:10:08.456 --> 00:10:12.623
and 33 years later, that's
a fully staffed company

00:10:13.544 --> 00:10:15.406
with books in translation

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:19.599
and hardcover editions
and the whole nine yards.

00:10:19.599 --> 00:10:22.457
But it started with, I'm
going to do my thing.

00:10:22.457 --> 00:10:26.374
I'll be City Lights
Junior, without the talent.

00:10:28.308 --> 00:10:30.273
I did that at the same time

00:10:30.273 --> 00:10:33.236
as I was doing my first spokenword gigs

00:10:33.236 --> 00:10:36.156
and why did I have the
gumption and the audacity

00:10:36.156 --> 00:10:37.783
to start my own book company?

00:10:37.783 --> 00:10:40.332
Because I come from punk rock.

00:10:40.332 --> 00:10:43.283
I come from watching Ian
MacKaye invent Dischord Records,

00:10:43.283 --> 00:10:46.027
on his Mom's kitchen table.

00:10:46.027 --> 00:10:50.874
I come from folding and gluing
picture sleeves together.

00:10:50.874 --> 00:10:52.604
Black Flag had their own label,

00:10:52.604 --> 00:10:54.270
of course you'd do it yourself.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.551
You want to make a book?

00:10:55.551 --> 00:10:56.843
Sit down and write it,

00:10:56.843 --> 00:10:58.211
then go to the printing place.

00:10:58.211 --> 00:10:59.440
Where's the printer?

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:00.613
Get the phonebook and find a printer.

00:11:00.613 --> 00:11:03.518
We make records, make some books.

00:11:03.518 --> 00:11:04.528
You just do it.

00:11:04.528 --> 00:11:05.411
It's like you don't even think about it,

00:11:05.411 --> 00:11:06.313
you just do it.

00:11:06.313 --> 00:11:07.777
I'm sure that writing is just awful,

00:11:07.777 --> 00:11:08.860
but I did it.

00:11:10.794 --> 00:11:12.338
And without even hesitation,

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:13.906
I'm making my own book companies.

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:15.573
Stop me, I dare you.

00:11:17.032 --> 00:11:18.679
I never thought about it twice.

00:11:18.679 --> 00:11:21.000
Just because DIY, man.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.500
Like, let your voice be heard.

